, us-west, eu-west) with an error rate of 18%. v2.3.1-rc had also been unhealthy in staging. Action taken: rolled checkout-api back in all production regions to v2.3.0, the most recent release that was healthy in production. After the rollback, the service is healthy on v2.3.0 in all three regions and the error rate is down to 0.2%. Follow-up: find the root cause of the v2.3.1 failure before redeploying it, and review why it was promoted to production after being unhealthy in staging.", "idempotency_key": "checkout-api-rollback-v2.3.1-to-v2.3.0", "confidence": 0.92}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled checkout-api back to v2.3.0 in all regions, and it's healthy again. I also opened SEV2 incident INC-0001, which paged on-call.\n\n- **What was wrong:** Since the v2.3.1 deploy, checkout-api had been failing health checks in us-east, us-west and eu-west, with an 18% error rate. The test build of v2.3.1 had also failed health checks in staging before it reached production.\n- **Rollback:** v2.3.0 was the most recent release that was healthy in production, so I rolled back to it (v2.2.9 is older). After the rollback, the service is healthy and the error rate is 0.2%.\n- **Incident:** INC-0001 says v2.3.1 failed, describes the rollback and the recovery, and lists two follow-ups:\n  - Find the root cause before redeploying v2.3.1.\n  - Review why v2.3.1 was promoted to production despite failing in staging.\n\nMy first attempt to open the incident timed out. I checked and no incident had been created, so I tried again with the same request ID (idempotency key), which means only one incident exists.", "type": "text"}]}]}